On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 16:08:27 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> One thing to note is that this ends up being very similar to the
> git-based architecture, but without using git to push/pull across
> machines.

Agreed; I touched on this at the end of my reply to the RFC [1]. The
parts map one to one:

- An agent's push to `submissions` becomes git send-email to the
  local SMTP server.
- Your merge into mail@upstream becomes the server's delivery into
  the maildir.
- The pull everyone runs to read the list becomes mbsync for you, and
  the delivered maildir itself for anything on the host.

What changes is the unit of sync. Git moves a whole tree and can
conflict when two sides both add mail; mbsync moves single messages,
and two sides adding different messages never conflict. That is the
real gain.

What is lost is the gate and the history, since both came from the
merge. History does not have to go with it, though. jj can still
snapshot the maildir on a schedule, or after each `notmuch new`, as a
record rather than a transport. Nothing would push or pull it, so it
could never conflict, and you would keep `jj log` over the list. The
gate is the open question from point 1 of [1], and I still need your
call on it before writing the spec.

Status: feedback only; no patch.
